#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:5
#: 8c534882ae634f228af6244a6d1c574e
msgid "Sensor Drivers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:7
#: 77e6e84b80304fceb1817be970597673
msgid "ArduPilot supports a wide variety of sensors from many different manufacturers.  One clear example of this can be seen in the :ref:`list of range finders <copter:common-rangefinder-landingpage>` (aka sonars, lidars). This page attempts to explain how sensor drivers are written and integrated into the vehicle code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:11
#: e036d6c7b2ff40019cff54aecaff9821
msgid "Supported Protocols"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:13
#: c0b82b321d944943a49c903946a5d04b
msgid "`I2C <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I%C2%B2C>`__, `SPI <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serial_Peripheral_Interface_Bus>`__, `UART (aka Serial) <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_asynchronous_receiver/transmitter>`__ and `CANBUS <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CAN_bus>`__ (in particular UAVCAN/DroneCAN) protocols are supported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:16
#: 5ef8cd224d3741889ef53bcf22e3d10c
msgid "If you plan to write a new driver, you will likely need to refer to the sensor's datasheet in order to determine which protocol it uses. Alternatively, you could utilize an intermediary node to move the necessary data back to the autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:19
#: 9e2ac15612d74b3da644590a288703af
msgid "I2C"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:21
#: c520959cce9448089d501cffdeb0315e
msgid "one master, many slaves possible"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:22
#: 45c3c5ea36334eeb81b45e53fd3b5f8d
msgid "a relatively simple protocol which is good for communicating over short-distances (i.e. less than 1m)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:23
#: 75fd80af2f134669985b837c2fe7bca9
msgid "bus runs at 100kHz or 400kHz but the data rate is relatively low compared to other protocols."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:24
#: 94054fb80d6d4aacb235c337027d26f1
msgid "only 4 pins are required (VCC, GND, SDA, SCL)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:30
#: 19cfa529e0bc4066acf3fd72c23935aa
msgid "SPI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:32
#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:43
#: 7bb10a3820644ed9ae077680700e1c3f
#: a5aef80e5b0d4c4ca3c6eee41a945502
msgid "one master, one slave"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:33
#: 77638ca249d9415e905421710a956781
msgid "20Mhz+ speed meaning it is very fast especially compared to I2C"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:34
#: 35bf683468c44243bb2630753708f6db
msgid "only works over short distances (10cm)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:35
#: 4aea2b00ee22488f8295865e1a58848c
msgid "requires at least 5 pins (VCC, GND, SCLK, Master-Out-Slave-In, Master-In-Slave-Out) + 1 slave select pin per slave"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:41
#: 1aebfba427944d30bb77f461d2d7af46
msgid "Serial / UART"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:44
#: 48468c0ab9224ec9a3aa5b74bf41ff0e
msgid "character based protocol good for communicating over longer distances compared to I2C and SPI (i.e. 1m)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:45
#: 20e33904e05c4d0eb8191e480d5f0c79
msgid "relatively fast at 57Kbps ~ 1.5Mbps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:46
#: 695efdae1b214ec1a657c0177631cdca
msgid "at least 4 pins required (VCC, GND, TX, RX), plus 2 optional pins (Clear-To-Send, Clear-To-Receive)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:49
#: 473a7fd7027543baa226385bf53918ed
msgid "CAN bus with UAVCAN"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:51
#: bcdda6644380431691b3f67076070872
msgid "multimaster bus, any node can initiate transmission of data when they need to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:52
#: aead31b9eb4546afb1f55cacead4ff22
msgid "packet based protocol for very long distances"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:53
#: 112f3bb47aed4ac8b6ecb52d2a3aeb72
msgid "high speed, typically 1 Mb (however only 50% of the bus bitrate can really be used without major collisions)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:54
#: dd35a9970bf44ed9bd80169456198f60
msgid "at least 3 pins required (GND, CAN HI, CAN LO). Optionally VCC can be used to power nodes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:55
#: 394ae1dcdcfc45cc9842b11b05123352
msgid "point-to-point topology. Star or stubs topolgy is not advised"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:56
#: be865c86ddff43c99855c8beb5b07ef1
msgid "termination is required at each end of the bus"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:61
#: 40076ded2474413b869b925651596f49
msgid "FrontEnd / BackEnd Split"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:63
#: cb902a46a56343dc89270f1eae815b40
msgid "An important concept within the sensor driver architecture is the front-end / back-end split."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:67
#: c491a22daaf2421fa25b8aa0ce9492f4
msgid "The vehicle code only ever calls into the Library's (aka sensor driver's) front-end."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:69
#: bed5c8a2c410444da29111ad54cf8f18
msgid "On start-up the front-end creates one or more back-ends based either on automatic detection of the sensor (i.e. probing for a response on a known I2C address) or by using the user defined _TYPE params (i.e. RNGFND_TYPE, RNGFND_TYPE2).  The front-end maintains pointers to each back-end which are normally held within an array named _drivers[]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:72
#: a2157083dbff4aa59686bfc9f39f8792
msgid "User settable parameters are always held within the front-end."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:75
#: c830a8c66c8d4836b0d0e8d3de22804c
msgid "How and When the driver code is run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:79
#: cede19ab5f1c43ceb32e4c7a11107d2c
msgid "The image above shows a zoomed in view of the ardupilot architecture.  The top-left blue box illustrates how the sensor driver's back-ends are run in a background thread. Raw data from the sensors is collected, converted into standard units and then held within buffers within the driver."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:82
#: 6ae05b52b7e34735be2eb9102ee29bb1
msgid "The vehicle code's main thread runs regularly (i.e. 400hz for copter) and accesses the latest data available through methods in the driver's front-end. For example in order to calculate the latest attitude estimate, the AHRS/EKF would pull the latest accelerometer, gyro and compass information from the sensor drivers' front-ends."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:85
#: a36651dc1ffd4420a136c5ebc5b34d07
msgid "The image is a slight generalization, for drivers using I2C or SPI, they must run in the background thread so that the highrate communication with the sensor does not affect the main loop's performance but for driver's using a serial (aka UART) interface, it is safe to run in the main thread because the underlying serial driver itself collects data in the background and includes a buffer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:90
#: 0add7903541a4e5f9f830617875b0633
msgid "Vehicle Code and Front-End Example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:92
#: 79abcb4901944ebf9d260f872b16c59c
msgid "The example below shows how the Copter vehicle code pulls data from range finder (aka sonar, lidar) drivers. The Copter code's `scheduler <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/ArduCopter/Copter.cpp#L107>`__ calls the vehicle's read_rangefinder() method at 20Hz. Below is a picture of this method, the latest version can be seen in the `sensors.cpp <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/ArduCopter/sensors.cpp>`__ file. The rangefinder.update() method is a call into the driver's front-end."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:99
#: 6b22979363054c64849d74f0427f23bf
msgid "Below is the range finder driver's front-end `update method <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AP_RangeFinder/AP_RangeFinder.cpp#L289>`__. This gives the driver a chance to do any general processing it might want to within the main thread. Each back-end's update method is called in turn."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:106
#: b99c76f7f1994083b441e4ecff814f57
msgid "UART/Serial Back-End Example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:108
#: 817cd76c5be84873aed577692266119c
msgid "Next is the update method of the `LightWare back-end <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AP_RangeFinder/AP_RangeFinder_LightWareSerial.cpp>`__ using the serial protocol. As described on the `user wiki <https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/common-lightware-sf10-lidar.html#serial-connection>`__ the serial range finder can be connected to any of the flight controller's serial ports but the user must specify which serial port, and what baud rate is used by setting the SERIALX_BAUD and SERIALX_PROTOCOL parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:113
#: 1793da540387471b903f1d8059f84a5e
msgid "Within the serial driver's `backend code <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AP_RangeFinder/AP_RangeFinder_Backend_Serial.cpp>`__, it first finds which UART the user would like to use via the serial_manager class which looks for the parameters settings described above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:117
#: 0ef77ace541e4e1caf3dc9788d246fd1
msgid "Each time the driver's back-end update() method is called it calls the get_reading method which checks if new characters have arrived from the sensor and then decodes them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:119
#: 31ae632add844e5d898fe81163321f66
msgid "As mentioned above, because the serial protocol implements its own buffering, the processing of any data (see get_reading method) from the sensor is run here in the main thread. I.e. there is no \"register_periodic_callback\" like you will see in I2C and SPI drivers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:126
#: 5d5c99f5544b47a299ee894b39e16168
msgid "I2C Back-End Example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:128
#: a4d036dd606748369930cad319108586
msgid "This example shows the back-end for the Lightware I2C driver. In this case, the front-end gets the I2C bus and passes it to the back-end during initialisation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:133
#: 44cacde7e1c94fa2b9b6308345bddfe1
msgid "The back-end's init method then registers its \"timer\" method to be called at 20hz.  Within the timer method (not shown) the get_reading() method is called which reads bytes from the sensor and converts the distance to centimeters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:136
#: 541f149f85a246c7a16b8b73eb2c9dd0
msgid "SPI Back-End Example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:138
#: 32077556a8bb464d8b4ded8e55ce0938
msgid "This example shows the back-end for the MPU9250 IMU which includes a gyro, accelerometer and compass. The front-end gets the SPI bus and passes it to the back-end during initialisation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:143
#: a32c1b19f88c4b9e8726d4058151c6cd
msgid "the start() method is called during initialisation and configures the sensor. It uses semaphores to ensure no interference with other SPI devices on the same bus."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:146
#: c29f635d031046ebbe1d981b18fe1966
msgid "The _read_sample method is registered so that it is called at 1000hz.  Note there is no need to take/give semaphores within the _read_sample method because that is done as part of the periodic callback code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:148
#: 974285aa1a1d453486c1f8d78fef69a4
msgid "the _block_read method shows how data can be read from the sensor's registers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:153
#: 0048c47faf9f4921bcbdfd129de587b6
msgid "Additional Advice"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:155
#: 0954eeebdf264798a521358553bd781f
msgid "When writing a sensor driver, never include any wait or sleep code because this will either delay the main thread or the background thread associated with the bus being used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/code-overview-sensor-drivers.rst:157
#: 8863fddb87e54e2da693895625266eee
msgid "If a new library is written, it must be added to the wscript file in the vehicle directory (i.e. /ardupilot/ArduCopter/wscript) in order for it to be linked into the final binary"
msgstr ""
